// SWITZERLAND — Smartious city-level + country-level data
// Live online Cambridge IGCSE, A-Level, Pearson Edexcel, IB Diploma,
// and American AP for international-organisation, finance, pharma,
// and Swiss families across Geneva, Zurich, Basel, Zug & Lucerne,
// Lausanne & Vaud, and the Alpine resort belt.
//
// LEGAL POSITIONING NOTE — READ BEFORE EDITING. THREE HARD RULES:
// Switzerland is a CANTON-BY-CANTON market with NO federal right,
// and it is TIGHTENING. Supplementary is the default everywhere.
// 1. NO CONSTITUTIONAL RIGHT — the Federal Supreme Court held in
//    2019 (the Basel-Stadt case, 2C_1005/2018) that the Federal
//    Constitution's primary-education provisions confer no right to
//    private home tuition, that Art. 13 BV (private and family life)
//    creates no such right either, and that no right can be derived
//    from ECHR Art. 8 or its First Protocol. Even very restrictive
//    cantonal rules are therefore lawful. It is for each canton to
//    decide whether and to what extent home education is allowed.
//    State this first, always. Never imply a Swiss "right".
// 2. TWENTY-SIX RULEBOOKS, SPANNING THE WHOLE SPECTRUM:
//    - Notification-style (historically): Vaud, Neuchâtel.
//    - Authorisation required: Bern, Geneva, and most cantons.
//    - Teaching diploma (Lehrdiplom) required of the teaching
//      person: Valais, Fribourg, Graubünden, Lucerne and others;
//      in Zurich a person without a diploma may teach for at most
//      about one year, after which a recognised diploma is required.
//    - Effectively not available: Basel-Stadt, Zug, St. Gallen.
//    - Cantonal curriculum compliance (Lehrplan 21 / PER) is
//      required wherever home education is permitted.
//    Lucerne is a good illustration of what authorisation involves:
//    application to the education department by end of April,
//    teaching diploma, maximum four learners per teacher, the
//    cantonal curriculum, at least half the regular weekly hours
//    spread over at least three half-days on two weekdays, all
//    subjects of the level, twice-yearly assessment of the learner,
//    demonstrated social integration, roughly eight school weeks of
//    processing, and no cost coverage by canton or commune.
// 3. THE TREND IS TIGHTENING. Numbers roughly doubled in six years
//    to more than 4,000 children nationally (~0.2% of pupils), and
//    cantons — including previously liberal ones — have been
//    tightening rules, with the growth now reported as broken.
//    Say so; do not present Switzerland as a liberalising market.
// CONSEQUENCE FOR OUR POSITIONING — NON-NEGOTIABLE:
// Because many cantons require the TEACHING PERSON to hold a
// recognised Swiss teaching diploma and to follow the cantonal
// curriculum, an overseas online school CANNOT serve as a
// substitute for compulsory schooling in those cantons. Our clean
// default in Switzerland is SUPPLEMENTARY: the school enrolment
// (public, private, or international) carries the Schulpflicht,
// and Smartious teaches the Cambridge or IB track live alongside.
// Never claim we can carry the obligation; always tell families to
// confirm their own canton's rules with their cantonal education
// department. Figures cited are illustrative of scale and may have
// changed — hedge them.
// POST-COMPULSORY: compulsory schooling comprises eleven years
// under HarmoS (two kindergarten years, six primary, three lower
// secondary), ending around age fifteen; upper-secondary — Gymnasium
// or apprenticeship — is not compulsory. The A-Level years
// therefore fall outside the Schulpflicht. State it as a measured
// planning fact, NOT as a marketing wedge on the Hungary/Greece
// scale, because the compulsory years remain strictly governed.
// MARKET NOTE: Switzerland is the most expensive school market on
// earth. Day international schools commonly run CHF 25,000-45,000;
// the boarding tier (Le Rosey, Institut auf dem Rosenberg, Aiglon,
// Beau Soleil, Institut Montana) runs into six figures. Ecolint in
// Geneva is the world's oldest international school and the IB's
// birthplace. The fee gap is the largest in our entire coverage —
// use it, but respectfully: these are outstanding institutions.
// TIMEZONE: CET (UTC+1) / CEST (UTC+2 summer) — 1-2 hours behind
// Nairobi EAT, the standard European framing.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

